WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.759
HTTP headers can be set close to application code or farther out in the
delivery path.

2
00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:07.575
A Django project may use middleware and settings.

3
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:12.334
A Flask application may use response helpers, decorators, or after-request
hooks.

4
00:00:12.334 --> 00:00:17.764
A FastAPI service may use middleware, route-level response handling, or
dependency patterns.

5
00:00:17.764 --> 00:00:26.813
Infrastructure may also add or override headers through reverse proxies,
load balancers, API gateways, static hosting layers, or content delivery
networks.

6
00:00:26.813 --> 00:00:32.109
Global headers are useful for consistent defaults, but not every response
has the same needs.

7
00:00:32.109 --> 00:00:39.617
HTML pages, JSON APIs, file downloads, static assets, redirects, admin
pages, and error responses may require different policies.

8
00:00:39.617 --> 00:00:45.583
Route-level or response-specific headers can be appropriate when they
express a deliberate exception.

9
00:00:45.583 --> 00:00:54.565
The risk is fragmentation: if every route invents its own policy, teams
can end up with missing, duplicated, conflicting, or environment-specific
behavior.

10
00:00:54.565 --> 00:00:56.710
Header ownership should be explicit.

11
00:00:56.710 --> 00:01:04.486
If the application sets one policy and the proxy overwrites it, the final
browser-visible result may not match what developers reviewed.

12
00:01:04.486 --> 00:01:08.776
If both layers add similar headers, conflicts can make behavior confusing.

13
00:01:08.776 --> 00:01:15.077
If only production infrastructure sets the headers, local and staging
review may miss important differences.

14
00:01:15.077 --> 00:01:20.775
Ownership should define who sets the baseline, who approves exceptions,
and how changes are tested.

15
00:01:20.775 --> 00:01:24.194
A practical Python header program starts with an inventory.

16
00:01:24.194 --> 00:01:27.277
Which headers are emitted by application middleware?

17
00:01:27.277 --> 00:01:30.025
Which are added by proxies or hosting platforms?

18
00:01:30.025 --> 00:01:31.433
Which responses differ?

19
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:33.109
Which environments diverge?

20
00:01:33.109 --> 00:01:39.276
Once that map exists, teams can define stable defaults, document
exceptions, and create regression checks.

21
00:01:39.276 --> 00:01:42.896
The key is not whether headers come from code or infrastructure.

22
00:01:42.896 --> 00:01:47.320
The key is whether the final response policy is intentional and
maintainable.
